  "idleCompaction": false,
  "cleanupPeriodDays": 3650,
  "autoMode": {
    "environment": [
      "$defaults",
      "Organization: one engineer working on personal projects and small collaborations. Primary use: software development and AWS infrastructure.",
      "Cloud provider(s): AWS, SSO only, with no static credential files. Agent sessions set AWS_PROFILE=read and AWS_CONFIG_FILE=~/.aws/agent-config, which defines only the read-only `read` and `view` profiles, so a bare `aws` command runs as `read`. The `admin` profile lives in the user's own ~/.aws/config, is write-capable, and is used only when the user names it for a single command."
    ],
    "soft_deny": [
      "$defaults",
      "AWS Credential Escalation [named+specifics — **must name:** the credential, role, or profile being obtained]: Acquiring or switching to any AWS identity other than the read-only `read` and `view` profiles named in Environment. Any other profile counts regardless of its name, since profile names are agent-controllable; for roles reached through `sts assume-role*`, treat the role as write-capable unless `readonly`, `viewer`, or `audit` appears as a whole word or name segment and is not combined with `write`, `admin`, `power`, or `deploy`. Vehicles include `--profile`, `AWS_PROFILE=`, or `AWS_DEFAULT_PROFILE=` naming another profile; repointing `AWS_CONFIG_FILE=` or `AWS_SHARED_CREDENTIALS_FILE=` at another file; `aws sso login` or `aws sso get-role-credentials` for another profile; exporting `AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID`, `AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY`, or `AWS_SESSION_TOKEN`; `aws configure`; wrappers such as `aws-vault exec`, `granted`, or `saml2aws`; and editing ~/.aws/config or ~/.aws/agent-config to add or change a profile, `role_arn`, `source_profile`, or `credential_process`. When the identity is selected indirectly through a Terraform provider block, `.envrc`, a task runner, or container env, the bar binds at the step that runs under it (`terraform apply`, `direnv allow`, the `docker run`), not at writing the file. A bare `aws` command runs as `read` and is not this rule, unless an earlier step this session edited agent-config, `.envrc`, or the shell environment; identity-only checks such as `aws sts get-caller-identity` or `aws configure list` are not this rule either. The Standard Credentials exception does not clear this rule, because the SSO-only setup has no config-file credential to read. Consent clears this rule for the one named command only, never as a standing grant, and does not clear what that command does under the new identity, which still meets its own rule."
    ]
